.TH FBV 1 "2026-10-30" "fbv-ng 2.0.0"
.SH NAME
fbv \- framebuffer image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fbv
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIimage\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fbv
shows images on a Linux framebuffer console. It reads BMP, PNG, JPEG and GIF
files, and plays animated GIFs. The formats that are available depend on how
fbv was built.
.PP
fbv draws on the framebuffer device, so the kernel must provide one. This is
either a DRM/KMS driver with fbdev emulation (CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION and
CONFIG_FB_DEVICE), or a classic fbdev driver.
.PP
The user needs write access to the device. Usually this means being in the
video group.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alpha
Blend the image with the screen, using the alpha channel of the image.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-noclear
Do not clear the screen before and after displaying the image.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unhide
Do not hide the cursor while displaying the image.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-noinfo
Do not show image information.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-shrink
Shrink the image to fit the screen, when it is too large.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-colorshrink
Shrink with a color averaging filter.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-enlarge
Enlarge the image to fill the screen, when it is too small.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-widthonly
When fitting, fit horizontally only.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-heightonly
When fitting, fit vertically only.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-smartfit " \fIpercent\fR"
Cover the whole screen, as long as less than \fIpercent\fR of the image is
cut off.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-ignore\-aspect
Do not keep the aspect ratio of the image when resizing.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-delay " \fIdelay\fR"
Slideshow: show each image for \fIdelay\fR tenths of a second. A key press
stops the timer for the current image.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-imagename " \fInames\fR"
Names to show in the image information. For several files, separate the
names with `^'.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-orientation " \fIo\fR"
Initial rotation: 0 for none, 1 for 90 degrees, 2 for 180 degrees, 3 for 270
degrees.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-skiptty
Do not read keys. fbv shows each image and moves on: it plays an animation to
its end, and it waits for the slideshow delay.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-control " \fIpath\fR"
Accept commands on the Unix socket \fIpath\fR, see CONTROL. fbv then keeps
running until it receives \fBquit\fR. Only the owner of the socket can use
it. If another fbv still serves this socket, fbv does not take it over.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-graphics
Switch the active console to graphics mode while fbv runs. The kernel then
draws no text and no cursor over the image.
.SH KEYS
.TS
l l.
r	Redraw the image
< or ,	Previous image
> or .	Next image
space, enter	Next image
a, d, w, x	Scroll the image (cursor keys also do that)
f	Toggle shrinking
k	Toggle shrinking quality
e	Toggle enlarging
l	Toggle fitting the image horizontally
t	Toggle fitting the image vertically
i	Toggle respecting the image aspect
+, -, 0	Increase, decrease and reset zoom
n	Rotate the image 90 degrees left
m	Rotate the image 90 degrees right
p	Disable all transformations
h	Help and image information
q	Quit
.TE
.SH ANIMATION
Animated GIFs play with their own frame delays. A delay below 20 ms counts as
100 ms.
.PP
fbv follows the loop count stored in the file. A file without a loop count
plays once.
.PP
When standard input is not a terminal, fbv moves to the next image when the
animation ends.
.SH CONTROL
Each line sent to the control socket is one command. fbv answers with one
line: \fBok\fR, sometimes followed by values, or \fBerror\fR and a reason.
.TP
.B ping
Check that the viewer is alive.
.TP
.BR pause ", " resume
Stop and restart the animation clock. \fBresume\fR also goes back to normal
playback after \fBframe\fR or \fBprogress\fR.
.TP
.BI frame " n"
Show frame \fIn\fR of the animation, counting from 0, and hold it.
.TP
.BI progress " percent"
Move to the frame that matches \fIpercent\fR of the animation (0 to 100,
decimals allowed) and hold it. When moving forward, fbv steps through the
frames at the pace of the animation. When moving backward, it jumps.
.TP
.BI load " file"
Show another image or animation. If this fails, the current one stays.
.TP
.B status
Reply \fBok frame=\fIn\fB frames=\fIcount\fB paused=\fI0|1\fR.
.TP
.BR quit " [" retain ]
Exit and leave the image on the framebuffer. With \fBretain\fR, the console
also stays in graphics mode, for a program that takes over the display.
.PP
For example, with socat:
.PP
.nf
fbv -y -c -i -g --control /run/fbv.sock boot.gif &
echo "progress 40" | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/run/fbv.sock
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FRAMEBUFFER
The framebuffer device. The default is /dev/fb0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success and 1 on error. When a signal interrupts fbv, 128 plus the signal
number.
.SH AUTHORS
fbv by Mateusz Golicz and Tomasz Sterna; fbv-ng by Angelo Compagnucci.
